      title: 'Initial Presentation',
      content: 'A 50-year-old right-handed male auto mechanic presents to the PM&R clinic with 12 months of persistent right lateral elbow pain. The pain began insidiously without a specific injury and has progressively worsened despite 6 months of physical therapy (stretching and strengthening), a counterforce brace, two corticosteroid injections (the second injection 4 months ago provided only 2 weeks of relief), and activity modification. He describes sharp pain localized to the lateral epicondyle that radiates into his forearm extensors. The pain is worst when gripping tools, turning wrenches, and lifting heavy car parts. He cannot grip a coffee mug without pain. He drops objects frequently. His grip strength has declined significantly on the right. He is the sole provider for his family with three children, works 50-60 hours per week, and his employer does not offer light-duty options. He states: "I need you to put me on disability. I can\'t do anything with this arm anymore. My life is over if I can\'t work." His workers\' compensation claim was filed 8 months ago and has been contentious.',
