    const Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content: 'Key areas to assess:\n- **Pain characterization**: Location (lateral epicondyle, common extensor origin), radiation (into the forearm extensor mass), aggravating factors (gripping, wrist extension against resistance, lifting with palm down), alleviating factors (rest, avoidance). Night pain? (suggests significant tendinopathy or alternative diagnosis).\n- **Mechanism and timeline**: Insidious onset in a mechanic — repetitive gripping, wrist extension, and forearm pronation/supination are classic occupational risk factors. 12-month duration classifies this as chronic lateral epicondylitis (typical natural history is 12-18 months with eventual resolution in 80-90% of cases).\n- **Treatment history in detail**: What type of PT was performed? Eccentric exercises specifically? Were the corticosteroid injections ultrasound-guided or landmark-based? Steroid injection provides short-term relief but is associated with WORSE long-term outcomes compared to watchful waiting — the recurrence after his second injection is consistent with this evidence.\n- **Red flags and differential diagnosis symptoms**: Neck pain or radicular symptoms (cervical radiculopathy at C6 can mimic lateral epicondylitis), numbness/tingling in the hand (radial tunnel syndrome, posterior interosseous nerve syndrome), locking or catching (loose bodies, osteochondritis dissecans of the capitellum), medial elbow pain (medial epicondylitis), instability sensation.\n- **Bilateral symptoms**: Is the left elbow also symptomatic? Bilateral epicondylitis suggests a systemic or ergonomic component.\n- **Occupational details**: Specific job tasks, frequency of gripping and lifting, tool types, vibratory tool exposure, postures. Has an ergonomic assessment been performed?\n- **Psychosocial factors**: Workers\' compensation claim duration and disputes, financial stress (sole provider, 3 children), job satisfaction, fear-avoidance beliefs, catastrophizing. These are strong predictors of chronic disability and must be addressed.\n- **Functional impact**: DASH (Disabilities of the Arm, Shoulder and Hand) score. Specific activities impaired — work, self-care, recreation.',
