      content: '**Vitals**: BP 136/82 mmHg, HR 76 bpm, Weight 195 lbs, BMI 27.8.\n\n**General**: Well-appearing male. Favors the right arm. No acute distress at rest.\n\n**Right Elbow Examination**:\n- **Inspection**: No visible swelling or ecchymosis. No muscle wasting. Elbow carrying angle normal.\n- **Palpation**: Exquisite point tenderness over the lateral epicondyle at the common extensor origin, specifically over the extensor carpi radialis brevis (ECRB) origin. Tenderness extends 1-2 cm distal along the extensor mass.\n- **Range of Motion**: Full elbow flexion and extension. Full pronation and supination. No mechanical block.\n- **Provocative Tests**:\n  - **Cozen test (resisted wrist extension with forearm pronated and elbow extended)**: Strongly positive — reproduces his exact pain at the lateral epicondyle\n  - **Mill test (passive wrist flexion with forearm pronated and elbow extended)**: Positive — stretching the common extensors reproduces pain\n  - **Maudsley test (resisted middle finger extension)**: Positive — isolates the ECRB/EDC origin\n  - **Chair lift test (lifting a chair with forearm pronated)**: Positive\n- **Grip strength (Jamar dynamometer)**: Right 24 kg, Left 45 kg (47% reduction on the right). Pain-free grip strength on the right: 12 kg.\n\n**Differential Diagnosis Examination**:\n- **Radial tunnel syndrome**: Tenderness 3-4 cm distal to the lateral epicondyle over the radial tunnel — mildly tender (this is concerning for coexistent radial tunnel syndrome, which occurs in up to 5% of cases). Resisted forearm supination — mildly painful.\n- **Cervical spine**: Full ROM, no radicular signs. Spurling test negative. Normal C6 myotome and dermatome.\n- **Posterior interosseous nerve (PIN)**: No finger drop. Resisted finger extension intact. PIN compression less likely.\n- **Elbow instability**: Varus and valgus stress at 30 degrees of flexion — stable. Posterolateral rotatory instability (pivot shift) test negative.\n- **Medial epicondyle**: Non-tender. No medial epicondylitis.\n\n**Diagnostic Ultrasound (performed in clinic)**:\n- **ECRB tendon**: Thickened, hypoechoic, with loss of normal fibrillar echotexture. No full-thickness tear. Mild increased vascularity on power Doppler (neovascularity — consistent with tendinopathy). No calcification. No cortical irregularity of the lateral epicondyle.\n- **Common extensor tendon**: Partial-thickness undersurface tear of the ECRB origin (<50% thickness).\n- **Radial nerve**: Normal caliber at the radial tunnel. No compression or swelling.\n\n**DASH Score**: 62/100 (significant disability — scores >50 indicate severe functional limitation).',
