    const Section(
      title: '+ Discuss the differential diagnosis for chronic lateral elbow pain and interpret the ultrasound findings in the context of failed conservative treatment.',
      content: '**Comprehensive Differential Diagnosis for Chronic Lateral Elbow Pain**:\n\n**1. Lateral Epicondylitis (Epicondylalgia) — Primary Diagnosis**:\n- Degenerative tendinopathy of the common extensor origin, predominantly the extensor carpi radialis brevis (ECRB)\n- Histopathology shows **angiofibroblastic tendinosis** — NOT inflammation. The term "tendinitis" is a misnomer. The pathology is collagen disorganization, neovascularization, and failed healing response.\n- The ultrasound findings (hypoechoic thickening, neovascularity, partial-thickness undersurface tear) confirm the diagnosis and quantify severity.\n\n**2. Radial Tunnel Syndrome — Must Be Excluded**:\n- Compression of the posterior interosseous nerve (PIN) within the radial tunnel (between the radial head and the supinator muscle)\n- Can mimic or coexist with lateral epicondylitis. Up to 5% of lateral epicondylitis cases have concurrent radial tunnel syndrome.\n- Key differentiator: tenderness is more DISTAL (4 fingerbreadths distal to the lateral epicondyle over the mobile wad) rather than directly over the epicondyle. Pain with resisted supination is more suggestive of radial tunnel than epicondylitis.\n- This patient has mild tenderness over the radial tunnel — this warrants monitoring. If his lateral epicondylitis treatment fails, occult radial tunnel syndrome may be contributing.\n- EMG/NCS: Often normal in radial tunnel syndrome (a clinical diagnosis). EMG is more useful for excluding PIN syndrome (which causes motor deficit without pain).\n\n**3. Cervical Radiculopathy (C5-C6)**:\n- Can refer pain to the lateral elbow. C6 radiculopathy may present with lateral arm pain, weakness of wrist extension, and reduced brachioradialis reflex.\n- Excluded in this patient by normal cervical exam, negative Spurling, and pain that is precisely localized to the epicondyle with positive provocative tests.\n\n**4. Osteochondritis Dissecans (OCD) of the Capitellum**:\n- More common in young athletes (gymnasts, throwers) but can present in adults\n- Lateral elbow pain with locking, catching, or mechanical symptoms\n- Diagnosed by X-ray or MRI showing capitellar lesion\n- Excluded by absence of mechanical symptoms and normal ultrasound of the capitellum.\n\n**5. Posterolateral Rotatory Instability (PLRI)**:\n- Instability of the lateral ulnar collateral ligament. Can follow trauma, dislocation, or iatrogenic injury (e.g., from repeated corticosteroid injections, which weaken ligamentous structures).\n- Two corticosteroid injections raise mild concern for this complication. Pivot shift test was negative.\n\n**6. Intra-articular Pathology**: Loose bodies, synovial plica, radiocapitellar arthritis. Excluded by normal ROM and absence of mechanical symptoms.\n\n**Ultrasound Findings in Context**:\n\nThe ultrasound confirms **moderate tendinopathy with a partial-thickness undersurface tear (<50%)**. Key implications:\n- Tendon thickening and hypoechogenicity indicate chronic degenerative change, not acute inflammation — this explains why anti-inflammatory medications and corticosteroid injections have been ineffective.\n- Neovascularity (Doppler signal) is associated with pain generation. Some treatments specifically target neovascularity (sclerosing injections, high-volume injections).\n- The partial tear is significant — partial tears >50% thickness or complete tears may warrant surgical consideration. This tear at <50% is still within the range of non-operative management.\n- Post-injection tendon quality: Repeated corticosteroid injections are associated with tendon degeneration, collagen necrosis, and weakening. This patient\'s failed recovery may be partly iatrogenic from the steroid injections.',
