    const Section(
      title: '+ Compare PRP vs corticosteroid injection evidence, outline eccentric exercise prescription, and discuss when surgery is indicated.',
      content: '**Management of Recalcitrant Lateral Epicondylitis**:\n\n**Eccentric Exercise — The Evidence-Based Foundation**:\n- Despite 6 months of PT, the question is whether his program specifically included a **structured eccentric loading protocol**, which is the most evidence-based exercise intervention for tendinopathy.\n- **Protocol**: Tyler Twist exercise using a FlexBar (rubber bar). The patient holds the bar with the affected wrist in extension, then slowly allows the wrist to move into flexion (eccentric load on the extensors). 3 sets of 15 repetitions, twice daily, for 8-12 weeks.\n- The Tyler Twist protocol showed a 91% improvement rate in chronic lateral epicondylitis in the original RCT.\n- If the patient\'s prior PT did NOT include structured eccentric loading, this should be tried before advancing to injections or surgery.\n\n**PRP vs Corticosteroid Injection — Evidence Review**:\n\n**Corticosteroid Injection**:\n- Short-term benefit (2-6 weeks) in pain reduction\n- **Long-term outcomes are WORSE than placebo** — multiple RCTs and systematic reviews demonstrate that corticosteroid injection is associated with higher recurrence rates, delayed healing, and worse outcomes at 6-12 months compared to watchful waiting\n- Mechanism of harm: corticosteroids inhibit tenocyte proliferation, reduce collagen synthesis, and promote tendon degeneration\n- This patient has already had two injections with diminishing returns — further steroid injections are contraindicated and potentially harmful\n\n**Platelet-Rich Plasma (PRP)**:\n- Rationale: PRP delivers concentrated growth factors (PDGF, TGF-beta, VEGF) to the degenerative tendon to stimulate a healing response\n- **Evidence**: Multiple RCTs show PRP is superior to corticosteroid injection at 6-12 months for lateral epicondylitis. The Mishra (2014) and Gosens (2011) RCTs showed significant improvement in DASH scores and pain with PRP vs corticosteroid at 1-2 year follow-up.\n- PRP appears more effective than placebo in chronic cases, though the evidence is still debated in systematic reviews.\n- **Technique**: Ultrasound-guided injection of leukocyte-rich PRP (LR-PRP) directly into the area of tendinopathy. Typically a single injection with possible repeat at 6-8 weeks if partial response.\n- **Post-injection protocol**: Relative rest for 1 week, then graduated eccentric loading program. NSAIDs should be AVOIDED for 2-4 weeks post-injection (they inhibit the inflammatory healing cascade that PRP is designed to stimulate).\n- **Limitations**: Not consistently covered by insurance or workers\' compensation. Patient may need to pay out-of-pocket (\$500-1500).\n\n**Other Injection Options**:\n- **Autologous whole blood injection**: Similar rationale to PRP but simpler and less expensive. Evidence is modest.\n- **Prolotherapy (hypertonic dextrose)**: Stimulates a healing response through controlled irritation. Some evidence of benefit.\n- **Botulinum toxin injection**: Into the ECRB to reduce tension on the tendon. Temporary finger extension weakness is a side effect. Limited evidence.\n- **High-volume injection**: Saline + local anesthetic injected under ultrasound guidance to disrupt neovascularity and adhesions. Emerging evidence.\n\n**Surgical Indications**:\nSurgery is considered when:\n1. Symptoms persist for >6-12 months despite comprehensive conservative treatment\n2. Patient has failed structured eccentric exercise, at least one regenerative injection (PRP), and activity modification\n3. The functional limitation is significant (DASH >50, unable to perform essential job functions)\n4. Imaging shows structural pathology correlating with symptoms (partial tear on ultrasound/MRI)\n\n**Surgical Options**:\n- **Open debridement (Nirschl procedure)**: Identification and excision of the angiofibroblastic tissue within the ECRB origin. Gold standard with >85% good-to-excellent outcomes.\n- **Arthroscopic debridement**: Less invasive, faster recovery, allows evaluation of intra-articular pathology. Similar outcomes to open.\n- **Percutaneous tenotomy (TENEX/Tenjet)**: Ultrasound-guided percutaneous debridement using a needle-like device. Performed under local anesthesia in the clinic. Faster recovery than open surgery. Growing evidence base.\n\n**For This Patient**: He has failed conservative PT (assuming it included eccentric loading) and two corticosteroid injections. The next appropriate step is either (1) a structured eccentric loading trial if not previously attempted, AND/OR (2) ultrasound-guided PRP injection. If these fail after 3-6 additional months, surgical referral is indicated.',
