    const Section(
      title: '+ Discuss workers\' compensation for repetitive strain injury, ergonomic assessment, and your approach to the disability request.',
      content: '**Workers\' Compensation for Repetitive Strain — Key Concepts**:\n\n**Establishing Work-Relatedness**:\n- Lateral epicondylitis from repetitive gripping, wrist extension, and tool use in an auto mechanic is a well-recognized occupational injury\n- Causation: the mechanism (repetitive forceful gripping, vibration exposure from power tools, sustained forearm pronation) is biomechanically consistent with the ECRB tendinopathy\n- The workers\' compensation system requires documentation that the condition arose out of and in the course of employment — the repetitive occupational exposure is the proximate cause\n\n**Ergonomic Assessment — Essential and Often Overlooked**:\nAn ergonomic assessment of his workplace should have been conducted early in the claim but may not have been. Key elements:\n- **Tool assessment**: Size and weight of tools, grip diameter (tools with grips <3 cm or >5 cm increase grip force requirements). Are anti-vibration tools available? Torque-limiting tools?\n- **Posture analysis**: Sustained wrist extension, forceful gripping frequency, repetition rate. Overhead work requiring sustained elbow extension.\n- **Workstation modification**: Adjustable workbench height, rotating platforms to reduce awkward postures, tool balancers for suspended tools\n- **Work practice changes**: Task rotation, microbreaks, two-handed lifting techniques, power tool substitution for manual tools\n- **Personal protective equipment**: Anti-vibration gloves, ergonomic tool grips\n\nIf the patient returns to work without ergonomic modifications, the condition will recur regardless of treatment.\n\n**Approaching the Disability Request**:\n\nThis is a critical communication moment. The patient is requesting permanent disability for lateral epicondylitis — a condition that resolves in 80-90% of cases and has effective surgical options. Simply granting or denying the request would be a disservice.\n\n**Framework**:\n\n1. **Acknowledge his frustration and financial pressure**: His distress is real — he is the sole provider, he has been in pain for a year, the workers\' compensation system has been adversarial, and he feels hopeless.\n\n2. **Assess for yellow flags**: Fear-avoidance beliefs, catastrophizing ("my life is over"), passive coping strategies (seeking disability rather than active treatment), adversarial workers\' compensation claim. These are strong predictors of chronic disability and are potentially modifiable.\n\n3. **Be honest about the disability question**: Permanent total disability for lateral epicondylitis would be medically difficult to justify when treatment options (PRP, surgery) have not been exhausted. An IME examiner would identify this. Premature disability determination can harm the patient by removing motivation for treatment and locking him into a disabled identity for a treatable condition.\n\n4. **Reframe the path forward**: The goal is not disability — it is return to function. Present a clear treatment plan with defined milestones:\n   - Temporary total disability: appropriate NOW while undergoing active treatment\n   - PRP injection and structured eccentric exercise program: 3-month trial\n   - If insufficient: surgical referral with expected 3-6 month post-surgical recovery\n   - Ergonomic workplace assessment before return to work\n   - Graduated return-to-work with ergonomic modifications\n   - If all treatments fail AND surgery fails: THEN permanent impairment rating and disability determination is appropriate\n\n5. **Impairment Rating**: If permanent impairment exists after maximal medical improvement (MMI), rate according to AMA Guides to the Evaluation of Permanent Impairment (6th edition). Lateral epicondylitis with residual functional deficit would be rated using the upper extremity regional grid (class 1-4 based on clinical findings and functional loss).\n\n6. **Legal representation**: If the workers\' compensation claim is adversarial, ensure the patient has an attorney. The physiatrist\'s role is medical treatment and documentation — not legal advocacy.',
