    const Section(
      title: '+ Demonstrate how you manage the patient\'s anger, set realistic expectations, and maintain the therapeutic alliance.',
      content: '**Managing Patient Anger and Setting Realistic Expectations**:\n\nThis situation requires de-escalation, empathy, and honest redirection without capitulation or confrontation. The patient is not angry at you — he is angry at the situation, the pain, the insurance system, and the feeling of being disbelieved. The goal is to transform this adversarial moment into a collaborative one.\n\n**Step 1 — Do not become defensive. Absorb the anger.**\n\nI would pause, maintain open body language, and respond calmly:\n\n\'I hear you, and I understand why you are angry. You have been in pain for a year, the system has fought you at every turn, and now it feels like I am taking their side. I want to address that directly because it is important to me that you know where I stand.\'\n\n**Step 2 — Restate your position clearly and without apology.**\n\n\'I believe your pain is real. I can see it on the ultrasound — your tendon is damaged. I can measure it with your grip strength — you have lost nearly half your strength on the right. This is a legitimate injury and you are not faking it.\n\nBut here is where I need to be honest with you, because being honest with you is more important to me than telling you what you want to hear: putting you on permanent disability right now would not be the right thing to do for you.\'\n\n**Step 3 — Explain WHY in terms of his interests, not medical policy.**\n\n\'Here is why: you are 50 years old. If I write you off as permanently disabled right now, you are looking at 15-17 years before retirement age on a workers\' compensation check that is a fraction of what you earn. Your three kids are still growing up. Your identity as a mechanic — as someone who works with his hands and provides for his family — that matters to you. I do not want to take that away from you for a condition that I believe we can treat.\n\nThere are treatments we have not tried yet. PRP — an injection using your own blood to stimulate healing — has strong evidence for exactly your condition. If that does not work, there is a surgery with an 85% success rate. And if we get you back to work, we are going to make sure your workplace is set up so this does not happen again.\'\n\n**Step 4 — Validate the financial fear and provide concrete support.**\n\n\'I also hear the financial pressure you are under, and I do not take that lightly. Here is what I AM going to do:\n- I am placing you on temporary total disability for the next 3 months while we pursue active treatment. You will receive workers\' compensation wage replacement during this time.\n- I am prescribing a structured rehabilitation program.\n- I am scheduling you for a PRP injection.\n- I am requesting an ergonomic assessment of your workplace for when you are ready to return.\n\nIf after we have exhausted these options you still cannot work, I will be the first person to document that. But I owe it to you to try to get you better first.\'\n\n**Step 5 — Re-establish the alliance.**\n\n\'I know the insurance company has made you feel like the enemy is everyone on the medical side. I am not the insurance company. I am your doctor. My job is to get you out of pain and back to the life you want. I cannot do that if we are fighting each other. Can we work together on this?\'\n\n**Step 6 — Check in.**\n\nWait for his response. If he remains angry, acknowledge it again: \'I understand this is not what you wanted to hear today. You do not have to agree with me right now. But I want you to think about what I have said, and I will see you next week. I am not going anywhere.\'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: De-escalation through active listening, refusing to become adversarial, honest communication that serves the patient\'s long-term interests, reframing disability as a last resort rather than a first option, providing concrete financial protection (temporary disability), and explicitly rebuilding the therapeutic alliance.',
